{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The decision problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome has 100% probability), so the reference value is zero. The differences between each outcome and the reference value (i.e., the outcomes themselves) are then weighted by their probabilities to compute a mean difference for each lottery. For Lottery A: (4000 - 0)*0.25 + (2000 - 0)*0.25 + (0 - 0)*0.50 = 1000 + 500 + 0 = 1500. For Lottery B: (6000 - 0)*0.25 + (0 - 0)*0.75 = 1500 + 0 = 1500. The mean differences are equal (1500 for both), so the decision reason would be indifferent between the two lotteries.",
