  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing mean differences from a reference value (zero in this case) and comparing expected deviations. The individual does consider outcomes and their probabilities, and implicitly evaluates gains (since reference is zero), but there is no indication that they computed or focused on 'differences from a reference value' as a core part of their reasoning. Their justification is more aligned with a risk-reward trade-off and a psychological willingness to take a chance (YOLO), rather than a systematic evaluation of mean differences. While some elements (outcomes, probabilities) overlap with the reason's inputs, the core mechanism—evaluating mean differences from a reference—is not reflected in the verbal report. The justification is more heuristic and emotionally driven rather than rule-based around deviation from a reference. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual applied the specified decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

